{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310818,"nonce":"XK2gwj98a/GoDMNjZcZsVsJ4lh8=","title":"In-guest wasm parallel matmul via persistent worker pool — prefill+decode pooled, 3.8-4.5x over sequential","message":"In-guest wasm parallel quant-matmul landed (2026-06-15). Path: `RAYZOR_WASM_NO_HOST_MATMUL=1` (or any pure-wasm host with no native pool). Prior state was single-threaded (~7 tok/s decode); now ~30 tok/s decode-only, prefill 4.5x.\n\n**Architecture — persistent worker pool in the wasm host (`compiler/src/codegen/wasm_runner.rs`):**\n- `rayzor_thread_spawn` was rewritten from per-call Store+Instance creation → a lazily-built `WorkerPool`: N OS threads, each instantiates the module ONCE (sharing the one `SharedMemory` as `env.memory`) and reserves its OWN 1MiB stack ONCE (calls guest `rayzor_malloc`, sets that instance's `__stack_pointer`), then loops on an `mpsc::Receiver\u003cJob\u003e`. Pool size = `available_parallelism().clamp(2,8)`. **Per-call instantiation was the bottleneck** — per-spawn version gave only ~1.26x (instantiation+thread-create eats the parallelism); pooled gives 3.8-4.2x. This mirrors the browser's persistent Web-Worker model (rayzor_worker.js — each worker its own instance sharing memory).\n- `Job{fn_idx,env_ptr,slot:Arc\u003cJobSlot\u003e}`; `JobSlot{Mutex\u003cOption\u003ci64\u003e\u003e,Condvar}`. spawn dispatches round-robin + records the slot in `thread_handles`. join blocks on the slot's condvar.\n- TWO join host imports: `rayzor_thread_join` (boxes result as DynamicValue* — the `Thread\u003cT\u003e.join()` contract, used by the spike) and NEW `rayzor_thread_join_void` (barrier only, no box — the matmul uses this to avoid a per-join box LEAK; `host_alloc`→guest malloc, never unboxed).\n- `__stack_pointer` is now EXPORTED from the linked module (`wasm_linker.rs`, runtime global 0 → `rt_global_remap[0]`) so workers can set per-thread SP. Linker already exported `memory` + `__indirect_function_table`.\n\n**Guest side (`runtime-wasm/src/qtensor.rs`, `rayzor_tensor_matmul_qt_t_f32_threaded`):**\n- Prequantize x→Q8_K for ALL `batch` rows ONCE on the calling thread, then split output rows `[0,n)` into `nthreads` bands; each band → `rayzor_thread_spawn(fn_idx, BandWork*)`. `rayzor_qmatmul_band_worker` (table fn, address-taken so it's in the merged `__indirect_function_table`) reads its `BandWork` and runs `qmatmul_band_dot` — **allocation-free** (no libc/TLS on workers; workers' TLS is unset, so any malloc would collide — that's why prep is once-on-main + workers only dot). Bit-identical to sequential (same per-row reduction order → greedy MATCH preserved).\n- **Both prefill AND decode pool** (batch==1 gate was REMOVED after user asked \"is prefill morselised/pooled?\" — it wasn't; in-guest prefill fell to sequential `qmatmul_chunk_impl`). band-dot loops `for n_idx { for bch in 0..batch }` (weight row reused across batch), mirroring host `host_parallel_qmatmul`.\n- Band count: `RAYZOR_WASM_GUEST_THREADS` env (read once via OnceLock; WASI `inherit_env` passes host env) \u003e caller hint \u003e 6 default. `=1` forces sequential.\n\n**Measured (M1, Llama 3.2 1B Q4_K_M, settled machine, greedy):**\n- decode step_p50: sequential 137ms → pooled-6 35.75ms → pooled-8 33ms (~4.1x)\n- prefill_fwd_s (61 prompt tok): sequential 8.44s → pooled 1.86s (4.5x)\n- NOTE: in-guest pooled decode (33ms) now BEATS the host-pool path (78.75ms) by 2.4x. Native MIR unaffected (9.5ms/step). Don't conflate: host-pool path = native NEON over shared mem (`host_parallel_qmatmul`); in-guest path = wasm SIMD relaxed_dot→SDOT (via wasmtime fork) × pool.\n\n**ATTENTION also parallelized (commit 3d8bec8).** After the matmul pool landed, sustained decode capped at 16.7 tok/s (step_p50=58ms) on a 1200-tok run while SHORT-context was ~35ms (~30 tok/s). Diagnosis (empirical + a verification workflow, both high-confidence): the step GROWS with KV depth — isolated by generating a fixed burst after a short vs long prompt: ctx~18 → 35ms, ctx~1211 → 78ms (same fixed-dim matmul work; the +43ms is purely O(cache_len) ATTENTION). The matmul floor (~35ms) is fine; matmul dispatch is FLAT (~7-8ms, not the bottleneck). On the wasm in-guest path the native flash plugin (.dylib) doesn't load, so `flashAttnDecode`→`rayzor_tensor_flash_attn_decode_f32` (runtime-wasm/src/kernels.rs) ran a SEQUENTIAL q_head loop. Fix: split q_heads into bands across the SAME worker pool (each head's output is disjoint; each worker gets pre-allocated `scores` scratch → allocation-free). Result: ctx-1211 step 78→46ms; sustained decode 16.7→21.7 tok/s, step_p95 80→55ms. Gated cache_len\u003e=256. MATCH preserved. Same band+spawn+join_void pattern as the matmul.\n\n**Worker malloc IS TLS-safe** (tested: a pool worker doing Array.push + String concat returned correct results deterministically 3/3). So the Haxe attention fallback (bmm chain) COULD also be parallelized via rayzor.concurrent if ever hit — but the active path is the in-guest flash kernel, parallelized in Rust at lower overhead. `rayzor_tensor_bmm_threaded` still ignores its threads param (forwards to single-threaded bmm) — a remaining seam if the flash gate ever misses.\n\nRemaining headroom to 30 sustained: lower the ~35ms matmul floor (dispatch ~7-8ms via spin-wait vs condvar; or faster kernels) and/or Q8 KV (no wasm plugin yet). Branch `wasm-inguest-worker-pool`: 88f5843 (matmul pool) + 3d8bec8 (attention). Related: [[perf_wasm_host_parallel_matmul]] (host-side, default-on), [[bugs_wasm_linker_silent_unreachable]], [[perf_wasm_v8_vs_wasmtime_2x]] (relaxed_dot SDOT).\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `perf_wasm_inguest_worker_pool` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}